WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.380
Good to have you on fascinating frontiers, episode 102, its June 15th, 2026. New week, new discoveries.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:15.160
Let's see what's happening. Tiny red dots scattered across the early universe have turned out to be black holes cloaked in dense gas.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:21.820
James Webb Space Telescope Spectra revealed that one of these mysterious early universe red dots

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:25.940
is a compact object whose x-rays are absorbed by surrounding gas.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:31.980
The object's true nature only appears once the dense cocoon is accounted for in the data reduction.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:38.560
Researchers had wondered whether these abundant bright sources challenge standard galaxy formation models.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.840
The new observations instead place them on the evolutionary track of growing supermassive black holes.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.280
Follow-up programs will target additional red dots to test how common this disguise is at high red shift.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:59.760
The James Webb observations of one little red dot show a compact object whose x-ray output is almost entirely absorbed by surrounding gas,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:02.760
leaving only a red glow at longer wavelengths.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:09.760
This disguise explains why earlier surveys missed the true population of growing black holes at high red shift.

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:21.160
The same technique can now be applied to the hundreds of other red dots already cataloged, revealing how common such embedded phases were when the universe was under 2 billion years old.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:27.860
What fraction of today's supermassive black holes experienced a similar shrouded growth stage remains unknown.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.880
From hidden early universe engines to a very different kind of hidden motion, we turn to Venus's famously sluggish spin.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:46.440
Venus rotates once every 243 Earth days, so slow that a person walking westward at 6.5 kilometers per hour can match the planet's spin.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:52.940
The steady pace holds the sun exactly on the horizon, turning sunset into a permanent view.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.540
Planetary scientists note this works only because the atmosphere and surface move together at that latitude.

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:06.160
The effect ties into new giant impact simulations that could explain the slow retrograde spin.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:16.640
If Earth rotated as slowly as Venus, a single day would last 243 of our years, stretching sunrise to sunset across more than two centuries.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:29.100
On Venus, the surface itself creeps eastward at only a few kilometers per hour, so a walker moving westward at 6.5 kilometers per hour can stay exactly beneath the setting sun.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:36.400
That speed is roughly the pace of a brisk human stroll on Earth, yet it matches an entire planetary rotation.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:45.940
The geometry works because Venus's thick atmosphere and rocky crust turn together, letting the walker keep the terminator fixed without fighting winds.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:54.100
We can watch the same principle in fast forward during a lunar eclipse when Earth's shadow creeps across the moon at a few kilometers per second.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:02.740
No one yet knows whether Venus has always spun this slowly or whether a single ancient collision flipped and break it into the present state.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:09.680
While Venus offers a ready-made horizon chase, Europe is preparing to chase heavier payloads into orbit.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:16.560
The European Space Agency will stream the next area in six flight, which carries an upgraded booster configuration.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.820
Engineers have increased thrust and propellant capacity to handle heavier payloads on future missions.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.960
The launch targets a geostationary transfer orbit insertion that tests the vehicle's expanded performance envelope.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:35.980
Ground teams at Europe's Space Board in French Guiana have completed final integration checks.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.940
Watchers can follow the countdown and separation events on the agency's live feed.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:48.080
As Europe tests expanded launch capability, another long-running European mission receives extra time to keep working.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:57.920
The European Space Agency approved a three-year extension for the characterizing exoplanet satellite, allowing continued transit photometry of known worlds.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:04.800
The spacecraft's 30-centimeter telescope has already refined radii and densities for dozens of planets.

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:12.240
Mission scientists plan to target systems where atmospheric follow-up with James Webb or ground-based instruments is feasible.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:18.460
The extension keeps the observatory operating alongside newer facilities rather than retiring it early.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.700
Data releases will continue on the same public archive schedule.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:30.040
While Keops keeps watch on known worlds, a new concept aims to explain why some worlds look the way they do.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:40.800
NASA's early evolution explorer concept would fly a small telescope to measure precise radii and masses of planets near the 1.8 Earth-radius gap.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:52.120
The mission aims to distinguish whether photo evaporation or core-powered mass loss creates the observed divide between rocky super-earth and gaseous sub-neptunes.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:58.980
A single year of survey data could yield hundreds of new mass radius pairs across a range of stellar types.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:05.620
The draft proposal outlines a three-year prime mission with an extended phase for atmospheric characterization.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:11.780
For measuring planets we already know to harvesting what future explorers might use, we look at Titan.

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:20.620
A NASA-supported assessment concludes Titan offers unmatched stores of hydrocarbons, water ice, and nitrogen for future explorers.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:27.580
The analysis compares these reserves against the moon and Mars, where volatile supplies are more limited.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:33.660
Researchers modeled extraction methods that could produce propellant and life-support consumables on site.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:41.400
The study emphasizes that Titan's thick atmosphere and surface lakes simplify some logistics compared with drier bodies.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:48.380
Next steps include laboratory tests of cryogenic processing techniques using simulated Titan regolith.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:55.500
While Titan offers future resources, closer to home, a millisecond pulsar offers a near-perfect laboratory.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:06.660
China's 500 meter aperture spherical radio telescope found a millisecond pulsar spending 220 times per second around a companion star.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:14.920
The orbit's eccentricity is so low it ranks among the most circular measured, preserving a billion-year record of tidal evolution.

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:20.940
Timing data reveal the two stars have exchanged mass without disrupting the near-circular path.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:31.940
Continued monitoring while Titan constraints on the companion's mass and the system's age, the discovery adds a clean laboratory for testing general relativity in strong fields.

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:38.680
From one ultra-stable binary to the subtle imprint and entire galaxy leaves on gravitational waves.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:48.720
A new study shows the Milky Way's rotation imprints a subtle directional pattern on the nanohertz gravitational wave signal, from millions of binary stars.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:56.020
Future space-based detectors must subtract this foreground to avoid biasing measurements of the overall wave background.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:02.280
The signal arises from the galaxy's stellar population rather than rare catastrophic events.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:09.140
Modeling the spin component improves subtraction accuracy for both galactic and extra galactic contributions.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:19.200
Analysts will apply the correction once Lisa begins collecting data in the 2030s, from foreground subtraction to missing elements in interstellar chemistry.

00:07:20.020 --> 00:07:27.660
Diffuse interstellar gas contains the expected amount of sulfur, yet cold molecular clouds show only 1% of that abundance.

00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:35.640
Researchers propose the element locks into icy dust-grain mantles that current observations cannot easily penetrate.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:42.920
Laboratory spectra and new chemical networks are being tested against all the maps of sulfur-bearing molecules.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:49.360
The next round of observations targets edge-on clouds where grain mantles may be directly sampled.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:56.820
Laser-driven light-sales must withstand extreme acceleration while maintaining stability over interstellar distances.

00:07:57.820 --> 00:08:05.560
Thermal expansion, pointing Robertson drag, and sail wrinkling each imposed limits not captured in earlier idealized models.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:14.720
Engineers are now running finite element simulations that include realistic material properties at speeds above 0.1 times the speed of light.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:22.000
The updated calculations show that sail mass must increase to survive the journey, reducing the payload fraction.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:35.560
Laboratory tests of candidate meta-materials are scheduled for the coming year. A moon-sized body striking Venus at high speed and oblique angle could have reversed and slowed the planet's spin to its present state.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:44.480
The scenario reproduces both the retrograde rotation and the 243-day period without requiring later tidal evolution.

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:52.360
Hydrodynamic simulations show the impactors core would merge while its angular momentum dominates the final rotation.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.700
The model also predicts a temporary debris disk that could have influenced early climate.

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:03.120
Refined crater statistics from future Venus orbiters will test the timing of such an event.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:12.480
The UK Space Agency signed an agreement with VAST to study a mission that would send amputee astronaut John McFall to a commercial space station.

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:19.800
The study will examine physiological effects of long duration flight on a prosthetic limb and cardiovascular system.

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:27.740
McFall, a former Paralympic sprinter, would become the first person with a physical disability to live aboard an orbital platform.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:33.060
Data gathered could inform future crew selection criteria for commercial operators.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:38.380
A final go or no-go decision is expected after the initial feasibility report.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:48.680
The Hubble Telescope captured elliptical, spiral, and lenticular galaxies within the cluster, along with faint gravitational arcs from more distant background sources.

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:56.780
The arcs trace how the cluster's mass bends light from galaxies formed when the universe was less than half its current age.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:03.380
Photometric catalogs from the image will feed into studies of galaxy evolution inside dense environments.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:11.680
On June 15, Mercury stands 25 degrees east of the sun and shines at magnitude 0.5 in the evening sky.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:18.640
Observers can spot the planet half an hour after sunset, before it begins its rapid return toward the sun.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.720
The elongation offers the best northern hemisphere evening appearance of the year.

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:28.420
Binoculars or a small telescope will show the planet's small disk and phase.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:36.940
Dark skies around the June 14 new moon provide optimal conditions for spotting the three planets in the western evening sky.

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:42.500
Mercury, Venus, and Jupiter form a changing triangle that tightens through the month.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:48.380
Observers can track the planet's relative motions night by night without interference from moonlight.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:53.640
The configuration also places several bright summer constellations in good view.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:11:01.180
Before we go, keep an eye on the next round of James Webb follow-up observations targeting additional little red dots.

